1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,408
Algeria, deep in the Sahara.

2
00:00:02,408 --> 00:00:09,597
On the ceiling of a rock shelter, a giant figure with a smooth round head and no face looks down.

3
00:00:09,597 --> 00:00:13,154
For sixty years, people have asked one question.

4
00:00:13,154 --> 00:00:15,599
Is it an alien in a space helmet?

5
00:00:16,066 --> 00:00:22,157
Because buried in this story are a nickname, a bestselling book, and a long chain of copies.

6
00:00:22,157 --> 00:00:25,600
And somewhere along that chain, the picture changed.

7
00:00:26,066 --> 00:00:27,002
A real wall.

8
00:00:27,002 --> 00:00:28,405
A real expedition.

9
00:00:28,405 --> 00:00:30,277
And a joke that escaped.

10
00:00:30,277 --> 00:00:35,033
This is the caravan route to the so-called Great Martian God.

11
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:36,704
Tassili n'Ajjer.

12
00:00:36,704 --> 00:00:38,512
Evidence against theory.

13
00:00:38,512 --> 00:00:39,566
Let's set out.

14
00:00:44,033 --> 00:00:49,750
Day one begins in Djanet, a small oasis town in southeastern Algeria.

15
00:00:49,750 --> 00:00:58,533
From here, guides lead walkers up onto the Tassili plateau, a vast sandstone tableland cut by deep gorges.

16
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,525
Tassili n'Ajjer is a Tuareg name.

17
00:01:01,525 --> 00:01:04,663
It means the plateau of the Ajjer people.

18
00:01:04,663 --> 00:01:11,933
In nineteen eighty-two, UNESCO listed it, partly for its landscape and partly for its rock art.

19
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,634
The landscape alone is strange.

20
00:01:14,634 --> 00:01:21,336
Wind has carved the sandstone into towers, arches and narrow alleys, so-called stone forests.

21
00:01:21,336 --> 00:01:25,300
Walking through them can feel like walking on the Moon.

22
00:01:25,766 --> 00:01:27,056
Now meet the claim.

23
00:01:27,056 --> 00:01:35,746
In the nineteen fifties, a French explorer named Henri Lhote led a team here and came home with copies of hundreds of paintings.

24
00:01:35,746 --> 00:01:38,733
One figure, he called the Great Martian God.

25
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:48,627
Then, in nineteen sixty-eight, a Swiss author named Erich von Däniken printed that figure in a bestseller called Chariots of the Gods.

26
00:01:48,627 --> 00:01:52,566
His reading: a visitor in a spacesuit, wearing a helmet.

27
00:01:53,033 --> 00:01:55,212
The idea went round the world.

28
00:01:55,212 --> 00:02:00,587
Across all his books, von Däniken has sold more than sixty million copies.

29
00:02:00,587 --> 00:02:03,419
But which picture went round the world?

30
00:02:03,419 --> 00:02:05,453
A wall, or a copy of a wall?

31
00:02:05,453 --> 00:02:06,833
Keep that question.

32
00:02:07,299 --> 00:02:09,219
So we will do this properly.

33
00:02:09,219 --> 00:02:10,384
Walk to the wall.

34
00:02:10,384 --> 00:02:13,058
Look at what is actually painted there.

35
00:02:13,058 --> 00:02:14,977
Ask who painted it and when.

36
00:02:14,977 --> 00:02:18,199
Then follow the copies back out into the world.

37
00:02:18,666 --> 00:02:20,465
First clue, the climate.

38
00:02:20,465 --> 00:02:22,338
Today the Tassili is dry.

39
00:02:22,338 --> 00:02:29,533
But after the last ice age, the central Sahara was far wetter, with rivers, lakes and grassland.

40
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,571
Elephants, giraffes, hippos and rhinos lived here.

41
00:02:33,571 --> 00:02:40,500
Rock paintings and engravings show them, which is one reason researchers can tell the art is old.

42
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:44,500
Most of the big animals vanished as the desert returned.

43
00:02:44,966 --> 00:02:49,787
By about six thousand years ago, the Sahara had become much drier.

44
00:02:49,787 --> 00:02:50,737
Lakes shrank.

45
00:02:50,737 --> 00:02:53,659
People moved their herds south and east.

46
00:02:53,659 --> 00:02:56,800
And the high plateau became a time capsule.

47
00:02:57,266 --> 00:03:02,300
In the nineteen thirties, French soldiers on patrol noticed painted shelters.

48
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:09,099
One of them, Lieutenant Charles Brenans, made sketches between nineteen thirty-three and nineteen forty.

49
00:03:09,566 --> 00:03:16,494
His sketches went to a museum in Algiers, then to the great prehistorian Henri Breuil in France.

50
00:03:16,494 --> 00:03:20,895
And Brenans took a young ethnographer into the stone forests.

51
00:03:20,895 --> 00:03:22,699
His name was Henri Lhote.

52
00:03:23,166 --> 00:03:28,024
In February nineteen fifty-six, Lhote reached Djanet with a large team.

53
00:03:28,024 --> 00:03:36,166
Over sixteen months, they documented about eight hundred paintings, with painters and photographers recording each one.

54
00:03:36,633 --> 00:03:44,508
So the first day gives us a real place, a real expedition, and a nickname that was about to become a legend.

55
00:03:44,508 --> 00:03:45,456
Keep a tally.

56
00:03:45,456 --> 00:03:47,133
Now, on to the shelter.

57
00:03:47,599 --> 00:03:49,364
Day two is a long walk.

58
00:03:49,364 --> 00:03:56,960
The painted shelters lie deep inside the plateau, reachable only on foot or by animal, with guides.

59
00:03:56,960 --> 00:04:00,566
That is one reason so much of the art survives.

60
00:04:01,033 --> 00:04:03,076
The site is called Jabbaren.

61
00:04:03,076 --> 00:04:05,848
It is a Tuareg word that means giants.

62
00:04:05,848 --> 00:04:14,166
The rock here has eroded into domes and alleys, like streets in a stone town, with ceilings and walls to paint on.

63
00:04:14,633 --> 00:04:18,011
And on the ceiling of one shelter is the giant.

64
00:04:18,011 --> 00:04:20,454
Accounts differ on its exact size.

65
00:04:20,454 --> 00:04:27,066
Most put it at five to six meters tall, and it is among the largest paintings in the Sahara.

66
00:04:27,533 --> 00:04:28,442
Look closely.

67
00:04:28,442 --> 00:04:30,751
The head is a smooth round shape.

68
00:04:30,751 --> 00:04:33,479
There is no face, no eyes and no mouth.

69
00:04:33,479 --> 00:04:37,466
That absence is exactly what makes it look like a helmet.

70
00:04:37,933 --> 00:04:39,025
Next, the body.

71
00:04:39,025 --> 00:04:40,628
A broad, simple shape.

72
00:04:40,628 --> 00:04:41,647
Arms held out.

73
00:04:41,647 --> 00:04:44,196
Beside the head, a few small marks.

74
00:04:44,196 --> 00:04:48,566
The figure is huge, and the people painted near it are tiny.

75
00:04:49,033 --> 00:04:50,127
Now the surface.

76
00:04:50,127 --> 00:04:54,914
The paint is thousands of years old and has flaked, faded and cracked.

77
00:04:54,914 --> 00:04:57,377
Parts of the figure are simply gone.

78
00:04:57,377 --> 00:05:00,933
So any detail you think you see is partly guesswork.

79
00:05:01,399 --> 00:05:07,457
Von Däniken saw a visor in the head, a suit in the body, and antennae on the sides.

80
00:05:07,457 --> 00:05:11,033
Those are the claims we will test, one at a time.

81
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:16,943
Some figures nearby seem to float or swim across the rock, drifting in profile.

82
00:05:16,943 --> 00:05:20,250
To a modern eye, that looks like weightlessness.

83
00:05:20,250 --> 00:05:23,833
Researchers have suggested something very different.

84
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:34,775
Rock art researchers David Coulson and Alec Campbell note that floating figures could point to trance and out-of-body travel, which is part of shamanism.

85
00:05:34,775 --> 00:05:36,966
A spirit world, not a spaceship.

86
00:05:37,433 --> 00:05:43,877
Another scene shows women with raised hands approaching a huge figure towering over them.

87
00:05:43,877 --> 00:05:47,497
One scholar described a sense of fearless worship.

88
00:05:47,497 --> 00:05:50,466
That reads as devotion, not as a landing.

89
00:05:50,933 --> 00:05:56,159
The painters used natural pigments, mainly red ochre, on a sandstone wall.

90
00:05:56,159 --> 00:06:00,750
There is no metal, no glass, no technology anywhere in the paint.

91
00:06:00,750 --> 00:06:04,000
Everything in this picture is made from earth.

92
00:06:04,466 --> 00:06:06,457
So the question for day three.

93
00:06:06,457 --> 00:06:09,642
Is the Great God an oddball, or one of a family?

94
00:06:09,642 --> 00:06:11,566
The answer shapes everything.

95
00:06:12,033 --> 00:06:13,181
It is a family.

96
00:06:13,181 --> 00:06:23,207
On the plateau, researchers have recorded many figures with the same round, featureless heads, often painted in the same red ochre.

97
00:06:23,207 --> 00:06:25,733
The style has a name: Round Head.

98
00:06:26,199 --> 00:06:31,805
Round Head art shows people dancing, carrying sticks and bows, wearing arm bands.

99
00:06:31,805 --> 00:06:33,189
It shows ceremonies.

100
00:06:33,189 --> 00:06:35,126
These are not lone visitors.

101
00:06:35,126 --> 00:06:38,033
They are crowds in the middle of a ritual.

102
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:39,829
And some wear masks.

103
00:06:39,829 --> 00:06:45,013
In some paintings, figures wear the heads of animals, including Barbary sheep.

104
00:06:45,013 --> 00:06:49,600
A round head with nothing on it could be a mask pulled over the face.

105
00:06:50,066 --> 00:07:00,156
Archaeologist Jitka Soukopova has compared this tradition with masks, body painting and initiation rites across Sub-Saharan Africa.

106
00:07:00,156 --> 00:07:03,699
She sees ceremony and spirits, not astronauts.

107
00:07:04,166 --> 00:07:05,350
Who painted them?

108
00:07:05,350 --> 00:07:09,597
Hunter-gatherers living here when the Sahara was still green.

109
00:07:09,597 --> 00:07:16,699
Remains found in the region suggest they had dark skin and a sophisticated culture, including pottery.

110
00:07:17,166 --> 00:07:18,517
How old is the art?

111
00:07:18,517 --> 00:07:19,583
Dating is hard.

112
00:07:19,583 --> 00:07:23,849
Researchers use style, superimposition and associated finds.

113
00:07:23,849 --> 00:07:32,166
One study of sediments at the foot of the paintings suggests some Round Head art is at least nine thousand years old.

114
00:07:32,633 --> 00:07:38,133
The usual range is about nine and a half thousand to seven thousand years ago.

115
00:07:38,133 --> 00:07:43,281
Experts argue about the edges, but nobody argues about the neighbourhood.

116
00:07:43,281 --> 00:07:46,666
This art is far older than any spacecraft story.

117
00:07:47,133 --> 00:07:49,428
There is also a rule of scale.

118
00:07:49,428 --> 00:07:54,783
In Round Head art, important figures are painted huge and others tiny.

119
00:07:54,783 --> 00:07:56,007
Size shows rank.

120
00:07:56,007 --> 00:07:58,838
The giant is not a different species.

121
00:07:58,838 --> 00:08:01,133
It is a more important figure.

122
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:09,046
Soukopova counts at least seven of these central, towering figures in Tassili, and calls them Great Gods.

123
00:08:09,046 --> 00:08:14,933
Placed on the walls of chosen shelters, they look like the heart of a sacred place.

124
00:08:15,399 --> 00:08:18,494
The mainstream reading is therefore simple.

125
00:08:18,494 --> 00:08:24,611
Ordinary people in ritual dress, or spirit beings, painted by early Holocene hunters.

126
00:08:24,611 --> 00:08:26,914
The aliens are not in the paint.

127
00:08:26,914 --> 00:08:29,433
They arrive later, with the copies.

128
00:08:29,899 --> 00:08:32,185
So how did the wall become a book?

129
00:08:32,185 --> 00:08:35,545
Lhote's team did not just photograph the shelters.

130
00:08:35,545 --> 00:08:42,266
They employed painters to copy the paintings, and the copies were later shown as huge reproductions.

131
00:08:42,733 --> 00:08:47,404
And to see the faint paint more clearly, the paintings were sponged down.

132
00:08:47,404 --> 00:08:49,068
Wet rock brightens colour.

133
00:08:49,068 --> 00:08:54,700
It is a quick way to make a better photograph, and a terrible thing to do to a painting.

134
00:08:55,166 --> 00:08:58,537
Coulson and Campbell describe the result bluntly.

135
00:08:58,537 --> 00:09:03,903
Serious damage, and colours reduced to a travesty of their original splendour.

136
00:09:03,903 --> 00:09:08,100
The team's own methods helped destroy what they came to save.

137
00:09:08,566 --> 00:09:14,430
In two thousand three, British anthropologist Jeremy Keenan reviewed Lhote's publications.

138
00:09:14,430 --> 00:09:22,966
He concluded that many claims were misleading, that a number of paintings were faked, and that the copying was fraught with errors.

139
00:09:23,433 --> 00:09:26,659
Even without fakery, copying changes things.

140
00:09:26,659 --> 00:09:28,786
A faded patch becomes a line.

141
00:09:28,786 --> 00:09:30,839
A scratch becomes a feature.

142
00:09:30,839 --> 00:09:35,166
The copy is cleaner, bolder and more strange than the wall.

143
00:09:35,633 --> 00:09:42,078
And those big, clean copies were shown in Paris in nineteen fifty-seven and fifty-eight.

144
00:09:42,078 --> 00:09:48,376
The writer André Malraux called it one of the defining exhibitions of the mid-century.

145
00:09:48,376 --> 00:09:50,866
African rock art was now fine art.

146
00:09:51,333 --> 00:09:52,887
Lhote's book followed.

147
00:09:52,887 --> 00:09:55,783
In it, he gave nicknames to some figures.

148
00:09:55,783 --> 00:09:58,961
Was the Great Martian God a serious proposal?

149
00:09:58,961 --> 00:10:02,776
Coulson says he called such figures martians jokingly.

150
00:10:02,776 --> 00:10:06,166
Other accounts say he floated the idea in print.

151
00:10:06,633 --> 00:10:11,663
Either way, a nickname offered as a joke, or as a hunch, became a caption.

152
00:10:11,663 --> 00:10:14,993
And a caption can outrun the person who wrote it.

153
00:10:14,993 --> 00:10:18,799
Which brings us to the book that carried it to millions.

154
00:10:19,266 --> 00:10:25,345
Erich von Däniken's first book was rejected twenty-two times before it was published.

155
00:10:25,345 --> 00:10:28,063
Then it became a worldwide bestseller.

156
00:10:28,063 --> 00:10:34,000
Its argument: ancient peoples could not have imagined gods without help from space.

157
00:10:34,466 --> 00:10:36,411
That argument has a problem.

158
00:10:36,411 --> 00:10:40,022
It assumes ancient painters could not paint spirits.

159
00:10:40,022 --> 00:10:43,077
But cultures around the world paint spirits.

160
00:10:43,077 --> 00:10:45,855
Round heads, horned heads, animal heads.

161
00:10:45,855 --> 00:10:48,633
These are the gods of people who dreamt.

162
00:10:49,100 --> 00:10:50,900
So we can sort the claims.

163
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:54,708
Theory one: the figure shows a visitor in a space suit.

164
00:10:54,708 --> 00:10:59,209
Theory two: it shows a person in a ritual mask or a spirit being.

165
00:10:59,209 --> 00:11:03,433
Theory three: the copies made it look stranger than the wall.

166
00:11:03,899 --> 00:11:05,545
First the alien theory.

167
00:11:05,545 --> 00:11:11,053
A visitor in a suit, with a helmet and a visor, painted by awestruck hunters.

168
00:11:11,053 --> 00:11:12,412
It is a good story.

169
00:11:12,412 --> 00:11:13,700
It needs evidence.

170
00:11:14,166 --> 00:11:17,553
To test it, ask what visitors would leave.

171
00:11:17,553 --> 00:11:18,037
Tools.

172
00:11:18,037 --> 00:11:18,763
Wreckage.

173
00:11:18,763 --> 00:11:19,246
Metal.

174
00:11:19,246 --> 00:11:22,956
Anything made of materials that do not belong.

175
00:11:22,956 --> 00:11:28,197
Excavations at Tassili have found pottery, stone tools and bones.

176
00:11:28,197 --> 00:11:29,488
No alien object.

177
00:11:29,488 --> 00:11:30,133
Nothing.

178
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:33,516
And the helmet idea fails inside the art.

179
00:11:33,516 --> 00:11:39,491
The round head appears on figures of every size, with horns, with bows, with sticks.

180
00:11:39,491 --> 00:11:44,399
If every dancer wore a spacesuit, there would be a lot of astronauts.

181
00:11:44,866 --> 00:11:46,759
Second, the ritual theory.

182
00:11:46,759 --> 00:11:50,983
A round head is a mask, or a hood, or an idealised spirit.

183
00:11:50,983 --> 00:11:57,100
This fits the dancing, the animal masks, the body paint, and the ceremonial setting.

184
00:11:57,566 --> 00:12:01,279
But it has a gap, and honesty requires us to say so.

185
00:12:01,279 --> 00:12:04,920
Nobody can prove what a prehistoric painting meant.

186
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:09,133
The mask idea is a well-supported reading, not a certainty.

187
00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:11,147
Third, the copy theory.

188
00:12:11,147 --> 00:12:17,742
The strangest features, the neat visor, the floating look, grew as the paint faded and was copied.

189
00:12:17,742 --> 00:12:21,308
This is plausible, and Keenan's critique supports it.

190
00:12:21,308 --> 00:12:23,933
But we need the originals to know more.

191
00:12:24,399 --> 00:12:25,816
Now stack the three.

192
00:12:25,816 --> 00:12:28,082
The wall shows ritual and masks.

193
00:12:28,082 --> 00:12:29,639
The copies exaggerate.

194
00:12:29,639 --> 00:12:33,534
The book then added a spacesuit that was never painted.

195
00:12:33,534 --> 00:12:36,933
Each layer moves a little further from the rock.

196
00:12:37,399 --> 00:12:39,464
What could change the verdict?

197
00:12:39,464 --> 00:12:40,909
A dated metal object.

198
00:12:40,909 --> 00:12:46,277
A painting showing something we cannot explain with masks, spirits or animals.

199
00:12:46,277 --> 00:12:49,030
A document from the painters themselves.

200
00:12:49,030 --> 00:12:52,333
After sixty years of looking, none has appeared.

201
00:12:52,799 --> 00:12:54,448
Time to score the case.

202
00:12:54,448 --> 00:12:55,022
Day six.

203
00:12:55,022 --> 00:12:58,033
Evidence on one side, theory on the other.

204
00:12:58,500 --> 00:13:05,059
Round Head hunters painting it about nine and a half to seven thousand years ago: evidence.

205
00:13:05,059 --> 00:13:09,168
Masks, dances and ceremonies in the same style: evidence.

206
00:13:09,168 --> 00:13:12,700
The helmet as a plain round head outline: likely.

207
00:13:13,166 --> 00:13:16,069
The joke or the hypothesis: disputed.

208
00:13:16,069 --> 00:13:19,206
Copies that altered the record: partial.

209
00:13:19,206 --> 00:13:22,344
Spacesuit, visor, antennae: no evidence.

210
00:13:22,344 --> 00:13:24,933
Visitors from space: no evidence.

211
00:13:25,399 --> 00:13:26,272
The verdict.

212
00:13:26,272 --> 00:13:27,072
Not aliens.

213
00:13:27,072 --> 00:13:39,866
The Great Martian God is most likely a painting of a masked or spirit figure by early Saharan hunters, made about eight thousand years ago, and made stranger by its own copies.

214
00:13:40,333 --> 00:13:42,999
That does not make it less remarkable.

215
00:13:42,999 --> 00:13:53,733
It is one of the largest paintings in Africa, from a time before farming reached the Sahara, and it still holds a real mystery: what exactly did it mean?

216
00:13:54,200 --> 00:14:03,100
What lingers is the silence of that shelter, and the sense that people stood there eight thousand years ago and looked up in awe, just as we do.

217
00:14:03,566 --> 00:14:10,866
Next case: black triangles gliding silently over Belgium, seen by thousands, and chased by fighter jets.

218
00:14:11,333 --> 00:14:13,524
What do you think the painters meant?

219
00:14:13,524 --> 00:14:18,500
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
